description.narrative:
The Office of the High Commissioner for Human Rights (OHCHR) was established in Uganda in 2005 in response to human rights violations in conflict affected northern Uganda. The mandate was extended in 2007 to the entire country, and in addition to the office in Kampala, it has also had a presence in Gulu and Moroto respectively. In March 2023, it had 45 employees, of which nine were international staff. On August 5, 2023, the OHCHR in Uganda formally closed after 18 years of presence in the country. The Government of Uganda announced in February 2023 that it did not intend to further extend the mandate of the OHCHR office, forcing the office to close within six months. The closure of the office coincided with the end of the agreement with the Embassy of Sweden (2021-2023).
The OHCHR Uganda was supporting the Government of Uganda, national institutions and civil society to improve the realisation of the human rights of Ugandan people. Their country program built on the general OHCHR Office Management Plan (OMP) 2018-2021 (extended to 2023) and contributed to the joint UN Sustainable Development Cooperation Framework, 2021-2025.
The OHCHR Uganda program contained the following goals and results;
Goal 1: Strengthening rule of law and accountability for human rights violations;
Result 1. Laws, policies and practices are in line with international human rights obligations and increasingly address, prevent and reduce human rights violations in the context of law enforcement and justice systems Result 2. Strengthened national mechanisms to provide redress to victims and ensure accountability for human rights violations, including economic and social rights.
Goal 2: Enhancing equality and countering discrimination;
Result 1: Laws, policies and practices more effectively combat discrimination in all its forms Result 2: Women and girls, people with disabilities, people living with HIV/AIDS, people with albinism, indigenous people and LGBTI people are better able to attain equal human rights Result 3: Justice systems and related institutions increasingly monitor and investigate discrimination and provide redress to victims
Goal 3: Enhancing and protecting civic space and peoples participation;
Result 1: International, regional and national protection mechanisms are responsive to the protection needs of Civil Society Organisations and individuals, particularly but not only in the context of political participation Result 2: Civil society monitors systematically and advocates for civic space Result 3: Enhanced capacity of law enforcement and security agencies to adhere to human rights standards in policies, procedures and operations
Goal 4: Integrating human rights in sustainable development;
Result 1: National mechanisms and programmes increasingly are compliant with the international human rights norms and standards on business and human rights, including the UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights. Result 2: Government actors increasingly apply a Human Rights-based approach to maternal health in policy formulation, implementation and programmatic response Result 3: Increased compliance of UNSDCF and UN Agencies programmes and funds with international human rights norms, standards and principles Result 4: Increased compliance of State statistical framework including; National Standards Indicator Framework (NSI) and Results and Resources Framework (RRF) with international human rights norms, standards and principles related to data for national development
Goal 5: Increasing implementation of the international human rights mechanisms outcomes.
Result 1: Increased engagement by Uganda with international and regional human rights mechanisms Result 2: Government, Uganda Human Rights Commission (UHRC) and Civil Society increasingly engage with international human rights mechanisms
The OHCHR results framework details expected results, outputs and indicators. The annual plans further defines activities linked to each result and output.
